     </description>
-    <arg name="content" type="uint" enum="content" summary="packing and eye order"/>
-  </request>
-
+    <arg name="content" type="uint" enum="content" summary="packing and view order"/>
+  </request>
+
+  <request name="set_view_source" since="4">
+    <description summary="select the same native source rectangle in both views">
+      Select a rectangle independently within each view of side_by_side_full
+      content. Coordinates are native buffer pixels after the buffer transform,
+      relative to the top left of each half of the viewport source rectangle.
+      The viewport destination remains the logical size of one drawing area.
+      No new packing or view-order value is introduced.
+
+      The rectangle is double buffered with wl_surface.commit. Initially it is
+      unset. Set all four values to -1 to unset it. Otherwise x and y must be
+      nonnegative and width and height strictly positive. The rectangle must
+      fit inside each view when a buffer is committed; otherwise bad_source is
+      raised. With no viewport destination, the rectangle's size divided by
+      buffer scale gives the surface size. Fractional edges are retained.
+
+      Setting content to none or destroying the declaration also unsets the
+      rectangle. This request on a destroyed wl_surface raises no_surface.
+      Existing clients which do not set a view source keep their behavior.
+    </description>
+    <arg name="x" type="fixed"/>
+    <arg name="y" type="fixed"/>
+    <arg name="width" type="fixed"/>
+    <arg name="height" type="fixed"/>
+  </request>
 </interface>
 
-<interface name="kde_stereo_depth_v1" version="3">
+<interface name="kde_stereo_depth_v1" version="6">
   <description summary="desktop depth of one surface">
     The compositor shows the desktop's own surfaces at depth levels: the
     active window at the screen, the windows lying behind it sunk, menus,
     tooltips and popups popped. This object lets a client show depth
     inside a surface as well, by drawing the surface's elements once per
-    eye at their own depths and handing the two views over full side by
+    view at their own depths and handing the two views over full side by
     side, declared with kde_stereo_content_v1:
 
     - the compositor tells the client when the elements inside the surface
@@ -115,7 +141,13 @@
       stereo content;
     - a popup surface may declare its place.
 
-    The depth limits are the user's settings; the client reads them itself.
+    The depth limits are the user's settings, built on the display they are
+    shown on: since version 5 the compositor tells the client the limits of
+    the output the surface mainly sits on (depth_limits), the same limits it
+    applies to its own surfaces there, so that an element drawn inside the
+    surface at the popped limit and a menu the compositor pops lie at the same
+    depth on the same screen. Before version 5 the client reads the user's
+    settings itself.
 
     A surface may have any number of these objects. The depth state is sent
     to each of them. The surface is marked as desktop depth while any of
@@ -161,15 +193,17 @@
     <arg name="output_edges" type="uint" enum="edge" summary="the surface's edges on its output's edge"/>
   </event>
 
+
+
   <request name="set_desktop_depth">
     <description summary="Mark the surface's pair as the desktop's own depth">
       Nonzero marks the full side-by-side pair the surface shows, declared
       with kde_stereo_content_v1 by this client or its graphics library, as
-      the desktop's own elements drawn once per eye rather than stereo
+      the desktop's own elements drawn once per view rather than stereo
       content: where the compositor treats stereo content differently, for
       example in switching a display into a 3D mode, in stereo screenshots
       or in estimating the depth of content, the surface counts as part of
-      the desktop. The compositor still shows each view in its eye. Zero
+      the desktop. The compositor still presents each view separately. Zero
       withdraws the mark.
 
       Takes effect with the next wl_surface commit.
@@ -199,6 +233,45 @@
       wl_surface commit.
     </description>
   </request>
+
+  <event name="depth_limits" since="5">
+    <description summary="The depth limits on the surface's output">
+      Sent after depth_state once the object is created and again whenever
+      either value changes, such as when the surface moves to another output,
+      the output's mode or kind changes, or the user changes the settings.
+
+      sunk and popped are how far behind and in front of the screen the
+      compositor lets anything go on the output the surface mainly sits on,
+      in pixels of a view 1920 wide. They are the user's limits built on the
+      output's display: a headset gets more separation, a framed 3D screen
+      less (the display's kind and physical size, libstereo-policy). A
+      surface uses the limits for that screen through the shared
+      stereo_depth_limits_for_buffer conversion, never by its own width.
+      Since version 6, output_width is sent immediately before every
+      depth_limits event. Together they describe the same selected output.
+      The compositor sends both again when that output or its pixel width
+      changes, even when sunk and popped stay the same.
+    </description>
+    <arg name="sunk" type="fixed" summary="the sunk limit, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
+    <arg name="popped" type="fixed" summary="the popped limit, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
+  </event>
+
+  <event name="output_width" since="6">
+    <description summary="Pixel width of the output the limits are for">
+      Sent immediately before each depth_limits event to resources of
+      version 6 or newer. The width is the pixel width of one view of the
+      output the surface mainly sits on, the same output as the following
+      limits. It is independent of the surface, window and buffer sizes.
+      Zero means the surface has no selected output with a known pixel width.
+
+      One separation setting, centralized, tailored to the screen, not to
+      the element size: the person's choice, built on the display. Clients
+      pass this width and the following limits to the shared buffer-pixel
+      conversion; they never rescale depth by their own window's width.
+    </description>
+    <arg name="width" type="uint" summary="selected output width in pixels per view, or zero"/>
+  </event>
+
 </interface>
 
 </protocol>
